{"term":"骚味","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"sāo wèi","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"lewdness_quality","concept":"lasciviousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":70,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A sexually provocative or lascivious aura, quality, or smell attributed to a person perceived as sexually loose or lewd; the 'slutty vibe.'","english_term":"slutty vibe","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"骚 (sāo) carries strong connotations of sexual looseness and lewdness; 味 (wèi) means flavor, scent, or quality. Primarily applied pejoratively to women but can be used admiringly in some slang contexts. 骚 alone is one of the most loaded adjectives in this register."}
{"term":"浪","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"làng","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"slut_shame","concept":"wanton_behavior","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexually loose, wanton, or promiscuous; primarily applied pejoratively to women who behave in a sexually uninhibited or licentious manner.","english_term":"wanton / slutty","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literal meaning is 'wave'; in sexual contexts functions as a standalone adjective or verb meaning to act promiscuously. Appears in compounds 浪荡, 放浪, 骚浪. The pejorative is female-coded but can describe men. Very high recognition because the sexual sense is widespread in everyday speech."}
{"term":"浪荡","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"làngdàng","category":"desire_arousal_descriptors","subcategory":"dissoluteness","concept":"licentiousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":40,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Dissolute, licentious, and morally unrestrained; describes a person who drifts without purpose and indulges in sexual or moral excess.","english_term":"dissolute / licentious","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Has a non-sexual primary meaning of 'to wander idly' or 'drifting'; when applied to a person, strongly implies moral and sexual dissoluteness. More literary in register than 浪 alone. Can describe both men and women."}
{"term":"同性恋","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"tóngxìngliàn","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"homosexuality","concept":"homosexual","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":80,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Homosexuality or a homosexual person; the standard clinical and everyday Chinese term for same-sex attraction, functioning both as a noun for the person and as an abstract noun for the orientation.","english_term":"homosexual / homosexuality","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'same-sex love/attraction.' The dominant umbrella term used in clinical, legal, and everyday speech. The term itself carries no strong register stigma, though the underlying identity remains socially sensitive in mainland China."}
{"term":"男同性恋","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"nán tóngxìngliàn","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"gay_male","concept":"gay","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":80,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A gay man; male homosexual; the formal Chinese compound specifying a man who is sexually attracted to other men.","english_term":"gay man","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"More formal and clinical than 基佬; used in news media, academic, and official contexts. Frequently shortened to 男同 (nántóng) in informal speech. Nán = male; compound is symmetric with 女同性恋."}
{"term":"女同性恋","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"nǚ tóngxìngliàn","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"lesbian","concept":"lesbian","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":80,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A lesbian; female homosexual; the formal Chinese compound for a woman who is sexually attracted to other women.","english_term":"lesbian","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Often shortened to 女同 (nǚtóng) in informal speech. The community-preferred informal self-identifier is 拉拉 (lālā), a phonetic adaptation of English 'les' (from lesbian), which is warmer and more in-group than the clinical compound."}
{"term":"双性恋","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"shuāngxìngliàn","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"bisexuality","concept":"bisexual","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":3,"technical":75,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Bisexual; sexual attraction to more than one gender; the standard Chinese term for bisexuality and for a bisexual person.","english_term":"bisexual","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'dual-sex love/attraction.' Less frequently discussed in Chinese public discourse than homosexuality; recognition is somewhat lower among older demographics. Sometimes informally abbreviated to 双 (shuāng) within community contexts."}
{"term":"无性恋","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"wúxìngliàn","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"asexuality","concept":"asexuality","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":2,"technical":75,"recognition_pct_current":42,"recognition_pct_peak":42,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Asexual; a person who experiences little or no sexual attraction; the Chinese calque for asexuality as an orientation.","english_term":"asexual / asexuality","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'no-sex love/attraction.' Concept is far less broadly established in Chinese public discourse than in Western contexts; recognition concentrated among younger, internet-connected, LGBTQ+-aware demographics. The international AVEN community (Asexual Visibility and Education Network) has some Chinese presence."}
{"term":"跨性别","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"kuàxìngbié","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"transgender","concept":"transgender","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":3,"technical":80,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Transgender; the modern standard Chinese term for a person whose gender identity differs from their sex assigned at birth.","english_term":"transgender","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The preferred contemporary term in Chinese LGBTQ+ and academic discourse, displacing the older 变性人. Literally 'cross-sex/gender.' Recognition is growing alongside increased media coverage but remains lower than in English-speaking contexts due to the relative novelty of public trans discourse in China."}
{"term":"变性人","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"biànxìngrén","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"transgender","concept":"transgender","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"dated_slang","crude":15,"technical":65,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":2010,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'sex-changed person'; an older Chinese term for a transsexual or transgender individual, carrying strong implication of medical or surgical transition; now considered less affirming than 跨性别.","english_term":"transsexual","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"变 = to change; 性 = sex/gender; 人 = person. The emphasis on 'changing' sex rather than cross-gender identity makes it less affirming under contemporary frameworks. Still used in older legal, administrative, and media contexts. Was the dominant term before 跨性别 gained traction circa 2010–2015."}
{"term":"间性人","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"jiānxìngrén","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"intersex","concept":"intersex","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":2,"technical":88,"recognition_pct_current":28,"recognition_pct_peak":28,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Intersex person; a person born with sex characteristics that do not fit typical binary male or female definitions; the clinical Chinese term for intersex.","english_term":"intersex person","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Extremely low recognition outside medical and LGBTQ+ advocacy contexts. 间 = between/intermediate. Older terms include 雌雄同体 (hermaphrodite) and 阴阳人 (yin-yang person, archaic and pejorative). 间性人 is used primarily by activists and medical professionals; the general population rarely encounters it."}
{"term":"泛性恋","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"fànxìngliàn","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"pansexuality","concept":"pansexual","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":2,"technical":75,"recognition_pct_current":25,"recognition_pct_peak":25,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Pansexual; sexual attraction to others regardless of sex or gender identity; the Chinese calque for pansexuality.","english_term":"pansexual / pansexuality","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Very niche term in China; known primarily among younger urban LGBTQ+-aware demographics. 泛 = broad/pan. The distinction from 双性恋 (bisexual) is not widely understood in Chinese public discourse; the two terms are often conflated."}
{"term":"酷儿","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"kùér","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"queer","concept":"queer_identity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":10,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":28,"recognition_pct_peak":28,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Queer; a phonosemantic calque of the English word 'queer' used as a non-normative identity umbrella for LGBTQ+ people; the 酷 (cool) component gives it a positive valence absent from the English word's historical use as a slur.","english_term":"queer","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"酷 (kù) = cool/awesome; 儿 (ér) = diminutive/softening suffix. The combination approximates the sound of English 'queer' while reframing it positively. Used primarily by urban Chinese LGBTQ+ activists, academics, and community members; not widely recognized by the general population. The reclamation politics of the English original are largely lost in translation."}
{"term":"异性恋","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"yìxìngliàn","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"heterosexuality","concept":"heterosexual","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":2,"technical":80,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Heterosexual; the standard Chinese clinical and colloquial term for sexual attraction to people of a different sex; the default assumed orientation in mainstream Chinese discourse.","english_term":"heterosexual / straight","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'different-sex love/attraction.' Symmetrically formed with 同性恋. In everyday speech, heterosexuality is typically unmarked and assumed rather than named; this term is mainly used when explicitly contrasting with non-heterosexual orientations."}
{"term":"性别认同","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"xìngbié rèntóng","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"gender_identity","concept":"gender_identity","is_novel_concept":true,"register":"clinical","crude":0,"technical":90,"recognition_pct_current":50,"recognition_pct_peak":50,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Gender identity; a person's internal sense of their own gender, which may or may not correspond with the sex assigned at birth; the Chinese compound for this psychological and sociological concept.","english_term":"gender identity","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Direct calque of the English clinical/psychological term. More common in academic, activist, and policy discourse than in everyday speech. Recognition increasing alongside broader LGBTQ+ discourse in Chinese media. 性别 = sex/gender; 认同 = identification/identity."}
{"term":"非二元性别","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"fēi èryuán xìngbié","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"non_binary","concept":"non_binary","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":2,"technical":75,"recognition_pct_current":20,"recognition_pct_peak":20,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Non-binary gender; a gender identity that does not fall exclusively within the male or female binary; the Chinese translation of 'non-binary.'","english_term":"non-binary","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Very low recognition outside LGBTQ+ activist and academic circles in China. The concept of gender as a non-binary spectrum is far less established in Chinese public discourse than in Western contexts. Literally 'not-binary sex/gender.' 非 = not/non; 二元 = binary/dualistic; 性别 = sex/gender."}
{"term":"LGBT","lang":"zh","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"lgbtq_umbrella","concept":"queer_identity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":5,"technical":45,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The acronym LGBT (Lesbian, Gay, Bisexual, Transgender) borrowed directly into Chinese usage as an umbrella identity and community term for sexual and gender minorities.","english_term":"LGBT","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Used in Chinese text as the unmodified Latin letters 'LGBT'; sometimes extended to LGBT+ or LGBTQ. Recognition highest among younger, urban, and educated demographics. The acronym is subject to intermittent censorship on mainland Chinese platforms (Weibo, WeChat), driving use of euphemisms like 彩虹族."}
{"term":"彩虹族","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"cǎihóng zú","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"lgbtq_community","concept":"queer_identity","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":5,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":52,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Rainbow tribe/community'; a Chinese euphemistic community label for LGBTQ+ people, drawing on the rainbow as an international pride symbol.","english_term":"rainbow community / LGBTQ+ community","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"彩虹 = rainbow; 族 = ethnic group/clan/tribe. Used primarily in mainland Chinese media and online discussion as a soft or censorship-evading reference to the LGBTQ+ community. Less used as a self-identifier by community members, who tend to prefer 'LGBT' or specific identity terms. The rainbow framing is less politically charged than the English equivalent."}
{"term":"基佬","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"jīlǎo","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"gay_slur","concept":"gay_man_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":60,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Cantonese-origin slang for a gay man; widely used across Chinese-speaking regions as a slur but partially reclaimed as a neutral or affectionate in-group term within gay male communities.","english_term":"faggot / gay man","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"基 (jī) is a phonetic borrowing from Cantonese 基 (gei¹), itself a loanword from English 'gay'; 佬 (lǎo) is Cantonese/Southern colloquial for 'man/fellow.' Originated in Hong Kong; now pan-Chinese internet slang. Connotation ranges from hostile slur to neutral descriptor or even affectionate in-group label depending on speaker, context, and relationship."}
{"term":"死基佬","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"sǐ jīlǎo","category":"insults","subcategory":"gay_slur_intensified","concept":"gay_man_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":85,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Dead gay man'; a strongly intensified Cantonese-style slur for a gay man in which 死 (sǐ, 'dead') functions as an expletive intensifier conveying contempt or disgust rather than a literal reference to death.","english_term":"fucking faggot","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"死 here is a Cantonese expletive intensifier (as in 死鬼 = 'damn ghost'), not literal. More hostile and unambiguously derogatory than 基佬 alone. Primarily encountered in Cantonese-influenced contexts (HK, Guangdong, internet) but widely understood via internet spread. Not usable in any neutral or reclaimed sense."}
{"term":"娘炮","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"niángpào","category":"insults","subcategory":"effeminate_male","concept":"effeminate_man_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Girly cannon'; a pejorative for an effeminate male; widely used to mock men or boys who fail to conform to masculine gender norms.","english_term":"sissy / femboy (pejorative)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"娘 (niáng) = woman/mother (femininity); 炮 (pào) = cannon (crude/explosive intensifier). The compound implies exaggerated, objectionable femininity. Frequently deployed in Chinese political and media discourse against the perceived cultural 'feminization' (阴柔化) of Chinese men; the term has been used approvingly in state media commentary on masculinity norms."}
{"term":"娘娘腔","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"niángniang qiāng","category":"insults","subcategory":"effeminate_male","concept":"effeminate_man_slur","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":38,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Empress tone/manner'; a pejorative describing effeminate speech, mannerisms, or overall affect in a man; less aggressively offensive than 娘炮 but clearly pejorative.","english_term":"effeminate / girly manner","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"娘娘 (niángniang) = empress or very feminine woman; 腔 (qiāng) = tone, accent, manner of speech. Can refer specifically to a high-pitched or breathy voice. More descriptive in register than 娘炮 and sometimes deployed in ostensibly neutral observation, though the pejorative framing is the default reading."}
{"term":"人妖","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"rényāo","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"transgender_slur","concept":"ladyboy","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":78,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Literally 'human demon/monster'; a derogatory Chinese term for transgender women or gender-nonconforming people, particularly Thai kathoey; frames such individuals as supernatural aberrations.","english_term":"ladyboy (pejorative) / tranny","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"人 (rén) = person/human; 妖 (yāo) = demon, goblin, supernatural monster. Strongly dehumanizing framing. In Chinese popular culture and tourism contexts, primarily applied to Thai trans women performers with an exotic/spectacle framing; also used as a slur against Chinese transgender women. Widely recognized."}
{"term":"玻璃","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"bōlí","category":"identities_orientations","subcategory":"gay_coded_euphemism","concept":"gay","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"subculture","crude":20,"technical":0,"recognition_pct_current":45,"recognition_pct_peak":45,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Glass'; Chinese internet and subculture coded slang for a gay person, especially a gay man; a euphemism used where direct mention of homosexuality is sensitive or subject to platform censorship.","english_term":"gay","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Etymology debated; possible origins include metaphors of fragility/transparency or derivation via Shanghainese/Cantonese slang chains. Widely used on Chinese social media (Weibo, Douban) as a censorship-evading euphemism. Recognition skews young and internet-active. Also sometimes used as a self-identifier within gay communities without stigma."}
{"term":"断袖","lang":"zh","script":"hans","romanization":"duànxiù","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"male_homosexuality_euphemism","concept":"homosexual","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"classical_specialized","crude":5,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":60,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"'Cut sleeve'; a classical Chinese literary euphemism for male homosexuality, derived from the Han Dynasty story of Emperor Ai (r. 7–1 BCE) who cut his sleeve rather than disturb his sleeping male favorite Dong Xian.","english_term":"(male) homosexuality","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"One of the most culturally prestigious Chinese allusions for male homosexuality; the fuller form 断袖之癖 (duànxiù zhī pǐ, 'the habit of the cut sleeve') is used in more formal literary contexts. Often paired with 龙阳 (lóngyáng), another classical male-homosexuality allusion. Now primarily used in historical, literary, or ironic registers. Recognition skews toward educated speakers; younger demographics have lower familiarity."}
